The GLSL headers under include/FidelityFX/gpu/ are the closure of the #includes of the ten fsr3upscaler *_pass.glsl files
(ffx_core*.h, fsr1/ffx_fsr1.h for RCAS, spd/ffx_spd.h, fsr3upscaler/*.h). ffx_core_cpu.h and ffx_core_hlsl.h are only reached
through conditional includes; they are kept so the headers stay intact.

Local modifications
-------------------
1. src/backends/vk/ffx_vk.cpp, ffxGetInterfaceVK: fpSwapChainConfigureFrameGeneration = nullptr. The frame-interpolation
   swapchain (ffxSetFrameGenerationConfigToSwapchainVK) is not vendored; it belongs to the frame generation project.
2. src/backends/vk/ffx_vk.cpp, CreateBackendContextVK: vkGetBufferMemoryRequirements2KHR falls back to the core
   vkGetBufferMemoryRequirements2 when the KHR name resolves to null. The backend decides "dedicated allocation supported" from
   the extensions the GPU LISTS, not the ones the app enabled, and then called the null pointer (crash in ffxFsr3UpscalerContextCreate).
3. src/backends/vk/ffx_vk.cpp, CreatePipelineVK: a missing shader blob (permutation we do not build) returns
   FFX_ERROR_INVALID_ARGUMENT instead of asserting; with asserts compiled out it dereferenced a null blob.
4. include/FidelityFX/gpu/fsr3upscaler/ffx_fsr3upscaler_callbacks_glsl.h: rw_luma_history is declared rgba16f, not rgba8. The
   image is R16G16B16A16_FLOAT (LUMA_HISTORY_1/2 in ffx_fsr3upscaler.cpp); rgba8 made every store undefined and the validation
   layer warned on each accumulate dispatch (found by tests/fsr2_test.cpp, the first real dispatch).
Not from AMD, written here: tools/ffx_blobgen.cpp (below). AMD's src/backends/shared/ffx_shader_blobs.cpp and blob_accessors/ are
not vendored; ffxGetPermutationBlobByIndex() and ffxIsWave64() come from the generated ffx_fsr3upscaler_blobs.cpp.

Build integration (indra/llrender/vulkan/CMakeLists.txt)
--------------------------------------------------------
* llvulkan compiles ffx_vk.cpp, ffx_fsr3upscaler.cpp, src/shared/*.cpp and the generated ffx_fsr3upscaler_blobs.cpp.
  Public include directory: ffx/include. So the headers are <FidelityFX/host/...>, not <ffx/...>.
* AMD's FidelityFX_SC offline shader compiler is not used. tools/ffx_blobgen.cpp (target ffx_blobgen, built with the repo's
  glslang package ll::glslang, so no new dependency) runs as a custom command, compiles the ten GLSL passes to SPIR-V 1.5
  (Vulkan 1.2 target, -Os equivalent through SPIRV-Tools, with #include support), reads set/binding/name of every
  resource back out of the SPIR-V, and writes <build>/llrender/vulkan/ffx_fsr3upscaler_blobs.cpp with the FfxShaderBlob table.
  Identical permutations share one module: 44 table entries, 20 SPIR-V modules.
* Permutations built (FSR3UPSCALER_SHADER_PERMUTATION_* in src/components/fsr3upscaler/ffx_fsr3upscaler_private.h):
    always on : HDR color input, render-resolution motion vectors
    always off: inverted depth, display-size motion vectors, motion-vector jitter cancellation
    selectable: Lanczos LUT (the SDK picks it itself on 32/64-lane GPUs such as RDNA), FP16 (16-bit variant of 4 passes), sharpening
                (accumulate-sharpen pass). Wave64 maps to the same SPIR-V (not a GLSL option).
    Auto-exposure is not a permutation; it is a runtime flag.
  So the context flags must be FFX_FSR3UPSCALER_ENABLE_HIGH_DYNAMIC_RANGE (required), optionally _AUTO_EXPOSURE,
  _DYNAMIC_RESOLUTION, _DEBUG_CHECKING. _DEPTH_INVERTED, _DISPLAY_RESOLUTION_MOTION_VECTORS and
  _MOTION_VECTORS_JITTER_CANCELLATION make ffxFsr3UpscalerContextCreate return an error (to enable one, add it to
  ffx_blobgen.cpp, rebuild; call ffxFsr3UpscalerContextDestroy after a failed create).

How to use it (for VulkanFsrUpscaler, indra/llrender/vulkan/vkfsr2.*)
---------------------------------------------------------------------
Headers:
    #include <FidelityFX/host/ffx_fsr3upscaler.h>          // context, dispatch description, jitter, quality helpers
    #include <FidelityFX/host/backends/vk/ffx_vk.h>        // Vulkan backend: device, command buffer, image wrapping
Entry points, in order (see tests/fsr2_context_test.cpp for a working create/destroy):
    VkDeviceContext dc{ctx.device, ctx.phys, vkGetDeviceProcAddr};
    scratch = ffxGetScratchMemorySizeVK(ctx.phys, FFX_FSR3UPSCALER_CONTEXT_COUNT)    // caller owns this memory, zero it
    ffxGetInterfaceVK(&desc.backendInterface, ffxGetDeviceVK(&dc), scratch, size, FFX_FSR3UPSCALER_CONTEXT_COUNT)
    ffxFsr3UpscalerContextCreate(&upscaler, &desc)                 // desc.flags, maxRenderSize, maxUpscaleSize, fpMessage
    ffxFsr3UpscalerContextGetGpuMemoryUsage(&upscaler, &usage)     // VRAM for the 'upscaler' owner of the VRAM log
    per frame: ffxFsr3UpscalerContextDispatch(&upscaler, &dispatchDescription)
               command list = ffxGetCommandListVK(cmd); inputs/outputs = ffxGetResourceVK(VkImage, ffxGetImageResourceDescriptionVK(...), name, state)
    helpers  : ffxFsr3UpscalerGetJitterPhaseCount, ffxFsr3UpscalerGetJitterOffset, ffxFsr3UpscalerGetRenderResolutionFromQualityMode,
               ffxFsr3UpscalerGetUpscaleRatioFromQualityMode, ffxFsr3UpscalerSetConstant
    ffxFsr3UpscalerContextDestroy(&upscaler); the scratch memory must outlive the context.
Gotchas found while bringing it up:
* FP16 must be decided by the caller. The backend's fpGetDeviceCapabilities reports fp16Supported from the extension LIST, not from
  what the device enabled. After ffxGetInterfaceVK, replace desc.backendInterface.fpGetDeviceCapabilities with a wrapper that calls
  the original and then sets caps->fp16Supported = VkContext::fp16Math (done in the test).
* VkContext now enables, when the GPU supports them: shaderStorageImageWriteWithoutFormat / ReadWithoutFormat (the output image
  has no format qualifier), shaderInt16, shaderFloat16, storageBuffer16BitAccess, uniformAndStorageBuffer16BitAccess.
  VkContext::fp16Math is true when all of the FP16 ones are enabled. The shaders declare the matching SPIR-V capabilities.
* The backend keeps ONE static VkDeviceContext (ffxGetDeviceVK copies into a file-static), so one VkDevice per process, and it
  manages its own descriptor sets, samplers and a constant-buffer ring inside the scratch memory (FFX_MAX_QUEUED_FRAMES frames).
  It records its own barriers (addBarrier/flushBarriers, plain vkCmdPipelineBarrier, no synchronization2) from the
  FfxResourceStates given to ffxGetResourceVK, so the caller must pass the true current state of every image it hands in
  and put layouts back afterwards if it needs a different one. Its barriers start from that state (src access = SHADER_READ), so
  the caller must make earlier writes visible first; VulkanFsrUpscaler::dispatch does that (vkfsr2.cpp).
* The upscaler does not allocate dilatedDepth, dilatedMotionVectors and reconstructedPrevNearestDepth: the dispatch description
  takes them from the caller (ffxFsr3UpscalerGetSharedResourceDescriptions). VulkanFsrUpscaler creates them through a second
  backend context (FFX_EFFECT_SHAREDRESOURCES), so the scratch memory is sized for FFX_FSR3UPSCALER_CONTEXT_COUNT + 1.
* FfxFsr3UpscalerContext is 512 KB (FFX_SDK_DEFAULT_CONTEXT_SIZE uint32s): keep it off the stack.
* ffxFsr3UpscalerContextCreate compiles 11 compute pipelines (about 17 MB of VRAM at 640x360 -> 960x540).
